Exactly. Penetration is one possible event inside intimacy, not the definition of it.

The durable version is more like an ongoing physical channel: touch, teasing, flirting, lying together, kissing, wrestling, massage, eye contact, dancing, showering together, affectionate contact in passing, sexual tension that does not always have to “resolve.” When every intimate interaction has to culminate in sex, touch can start carrying an obligation. When play is allowed to remain play, the connection stays much larger.

And the trust piece is structural. If both people can say what they want, what they do not want, what changed, what they are curious about, and what scares them—without those disclosures becoming ammunition later—you get a much higher-bandwidth relationship. Honesty makes exploration possible; safety makes honesty possible; continued play keeps the system from becoming purely logistical.

So there really is a distinction between:

sex as an act → sexuality as a shared language → intimacy as the persistent connection underneath both.

And yes, the folder-name thing is funny on a second level. A directory called something absurd or mythological looks like flavor text until someone opens it and realizes, oh, no, they actually put the machinery in here. The joke becomes part of the indexing system.